    "result_text": "There's a single test class in the project, and it passes along with the full build.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked for a `capacityWarning` response field that is `true` when an owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50, which is unchanged).\n\nChanges, mirroring the existing `bulkSignupWarning` pattern to keep structural impact low:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `setCapacityWarning`).\n2. **New `CityCapacity` helper** \u2014 a small, self-contained class (one public static `approaching(owner, repo)` method) that counts owners in the same city (case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed, matching `RejectCityAtCapacity`) and returns `true` for a count of 40\u201349. It's a fresh cohesive unit with no surrounding class complexity, and it leaves `RejectCityAtCapacity` (the hard-50 rejection) untouched.\n3. **Three responders** (`RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RecordIdempotencyKey`) \u2014 one added line each, right beside the existing `setBulkSignupWarning` call, so every Owner response (GET, POST-created, and idempotent replay) carries the warning consistently.\n\nEach touched function stays at cyclomatic complexity 1 with a single added line, and no existing method or class grew in complexity. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the new `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` acceptance test.",
